To successfully secure your architecture, you need to complete two final steps: Validate the Bridge 
Integration (Smoke Test) and Lock the Active Workspace Cache for Copilot Pro.Because GMS is now the 
central nexus between your design tokens, your web apps, and your Unity game engine, these two final
tasks protect your codebase against future drift. They ensure that your pipeline catches structural 
errors immediately, and they force Copilot Pro to strictly follow your master layout architecture.Detailed
Breakdown of the Two Final TasksTask 1: The Monorepo Contract Smoke Test (pnpm ci:bridge-chain)Since your 
apps are highly interdependent, you need a deterministic validation runner at the root of your monorepo. 
This runner executes your build files in a strict order matching your dependency chart:Compiles the primitive
core styles (@trt/tokens).Triggers the GMS bridge translator (sync-master-bridge.js) to parse tokens, generating
layout-tokens.css and the Unity metadata map layout-matrix.json.Concurrently builds and type-checks CRM, WMS, 
and TPR against those newly emitted GMS assets.If GMS fails or misses an export key, the contract runner fails
your local or remote CI pipeline instantly, protecting production.Task 2: Locking Workspace Cache for Copilot 
ProGitHub Copilot Pro lacks server-side repository indexing configurations. It relies completely on open file 
tabs, your active workspace directory tree, and a local markdown instruction guide. Creating a dedicated 
rule-set ensures that when you edit any file inside CRM or WMS, Copilot Pro remembers to read the GMS bridge
variables rather than inventing raw pixel numbers.
